    "### Short Report ###\n",
    "\n",
    "To find the optimal learning rate t, I implemented a for loop with the function slightly adjusted (I lowered the max iterations to 1000 so that the function can compute quickly for all values of t). Having run this a few times and looking at the graph plotted, I estimated that t=0.25 or t=0.5 were the best learning rates. I proceeded to test the convergence and classification with these learning rates on the data with max iterations now set to 100000. I found that t=0.5 was quicker to compute, however depending on the randomised initial theta0, sometimes led to innaccurate results, putting all the data in class 1. Hence I prioritised the increased reliability of the classification from t=0.25, despite it being marginally slower.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our convergence is weighted significantly towards the first two classes. The function does not seem to classify any data points into the third class. This could be for example because some of the features of the data has a significantly smaller scale, and so it gets dominated by the other features, which are typical of class 1 and 2. In order to classify equally into all three classes, we may have to do some standardising/scaling of the data before running the function. We could also say that class 1 seems weighted higher than class 2, as there are significantly more points in class 1 than class 2 in our results. \n",
    "\n",
    "I would need further background knowledge about the chormium compounds we are trying to classify the data as to say whether our predicted labels are accurate or not. Under the assumption that all 3 compounds are equally common, I can say that our classification is not accurate, as the probability of 0 class 3 in 300 samples would be incredibly low. However, if I was to be told that class 3 chromium compound is extremely rare, and class 1 is the most common, my conclusion would be that our convergence is pretty reliable, as our classification reflects that. "
   ]
