   "id": "6bf3238d-5d77-468c-882e-0f2ce4a89aaf",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For question 4 I have chosen to use the Kmeans clustering algorithm from Scikit as used in problem sheet 4. I have used, and adapted some of the code from problem sheet 4 in order to make this function."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 69,
   "id": "b0386304-06df-4fc0-bf2b-846fd2143bb9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n",
    "def ClusterFeaturesFit(K, Xh, Energies, n_init=10):\n",
    "    '''A function that will take K as the number of clusters, Xh the data, \n",
    "    and the vector Energies: a vector of 3959 energy values at which the absorptions ˆx were measured. I have also put n_init as a parameter, so that the algorithm can be adjusted for speed or precision. The default is 10, so that we have precise clustering results.  \n",
    "    This function will look to cluster the data points with similar features. We can then use averaged data from each cluster\n",
    "    to compute the classifications more effictvely.\n",
    "    We are clustering the energies of each data point. kvec will be a vector of length 3959, where kvec[i] gives you the cluster number of the ith energy.'''  \n",
    "    Energies_ = Energies.reshape(-1,1) # making Energies a column vector\n",
    "    Xh_ = Xh.transpose()\n",
    "    X_clust = np.hstack([Energies_, Xh_])\n",
    "    kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=K, n_init=n_init) \n",
    "    kvec = kmeans.fit_predict(X_clust) # predicts which cluster each energy is in, returns a vector of cluster labels\n",
    "    lossk = kmeans.inertia_ # gives the loss of this clustering, by taking the sum of pointwise losses between each point and the centroid in that cluster\n",
    "    kvec_ordered = OrderClusterLabels(kvec, X_clust) # reordering the clusters in terms of lowest to highest energies\n",
    "    return kvec_ordered, lossk"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "389b0500-96f9-4308-9a20-194976ff15fe",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The following code used the framework from problem sheet 4, and has been adapted to find an optimal number of clusters K for our data. Knowledge obtained from this was used in my answer for question 5"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 70,
   "id": "7c9da438-14ef-495f-8d23-4cda1bad078e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "nruns = 10\n",
    "losses = []\n",
    "kvecs = []\n",
    "for K in range(2,21):\n",
    "    lossK = []\n",
    "    for i in range(nruns):\n",
    "        kvec, lossk = ClusterFeaturesFit(K,Xh,Energies,n_init=1) # here I have set n_init to be 1, so that the function is not too slow. We are also doing 10 runs of each one, so this is essentially just making the n_init explicit individual runs. \n",
    "        lossK.append(lossk)\n",
    "        kvecs.append(kvec)\n",
    "    losses.append(lossK)\n",
    "\n",
    "losses = np.array(losses)\n",
    "plt.plot(np.arange(2,losses.shape[0]+2), losses, '.')\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"$K$\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"$L(C_1,...,C_K)$\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
