    "In order to find the optimal learning rate t, I initially applied the same process as in Q3, where I created a loop to see which learning rates converged and which didn't. Having discovered that generally t=2/4/8 seemed like the optimal range, I wrote further code in order to more rigorously find the optimal rate among these 3. I ran each process 10 times with random Theta0 starting points, and recorded how many iterations the function took. I found that the average over these 10 was quite similar between 2/4/8, with 2 being marginally quicker, however I found that with t=4 or 8 the function sometimes was quite slow and hit the max iterations without converging, which wasn't the case for t=2. Hence I intially deduced that 2 should be our new optimal learning rate. \n",
    "\n",
    "Having used this learning rate to classify the data, I found that it didn't actually classify effectively and all points were classified as the first chromium compound. I experimented with a few different learning rates to see if there was a different t which produced a better classification and found that t=16 classified into all 3 chromium compounds. \n",
    "\n",
    "Looking at the image, it seems as if this updated method does classify the data more accurately. In our non-clustering attempt, we only classified into class 1 and 2, howeever our new clustering method not only produces a very similar classificaiton into 1 and 2, and then also manages to classify some of the 2s into 3s. \n",
